shaderc: WGSL loops come without continuing blocks

Safari 18's WebGPU (measured on iOS 18.7) reads zeros from a loop that enters a `continuing` block through `continue`:
the loop's results stay as they were initialized. tint writes nearly every loop of an optimized shader in that form,
with the loop's body in the `continuing` block, so on those devices the loops return nothing (a temporal resolve turned
the image black). The same loop without the block runs right there, as plain for loops do, and iOS 27 runs both forms.

The WGSL writer takes a new option, inline_continuing_blocks, which shaderc sets: the statements of a loop's
`continuing` block are written before each of the loop's `continue` statements and, where the body can run to its end,
after it; a `break if` among them becomes an `if` that breaks. A loop whose `continuing` block ends in `break if` keeps
the block when one of its `continue` statements is inside a `switch`, where that `break` would leave the switch.

